<%# The current user's watched card ids FOR THIS BOARD — the per-user half of the
    tile watch badge.

    This is rendered in a real request, so current_user is valid here. That's the
    whole point of the split: cards/_card is broadcast to the board stream (no
    session, current_user is nil) and therefore carries no per-user state at all,
    while this element carries all of it and is never broadcast.

    Scoped to the board being viewed rather than the user's whole watch list —
    ids for cards that aren't on this page are useless here, and shipping them
    would leak which other cards the user follows into this page's HTML.

    One query (a single pluck), fixed cost, independent of card count — pinned by
    BoardsControllerTest's flat-query test.

    Replaced by CardsController#toggle_watch so the board behind the modal updates
    the moment you toggle. `turbo_stream.replace` on a missing target is a no-op,
    so toggling from a context with no board page (a direct /cards/:id visit, the
    account Cards page) is harmless. %>
<div id="watched_cards"
     class="hidden"
     data-controller="watched-cards"
     data-watched-card-ids="<%= current_user.watched_cards.where(list_id: board.lists.select(:id)).pluck(:id).to_json %>">
</div>
